
  That split is why this isn't a one-line move: a new settings module has to pick a delivery mechanism the browser side
  doesn't currently have. Two questions matter before I design anything; here's the first.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the app decides [3mwhich[0m environment's endpoint it's using at runtime. This is the part that determines
  whether "changing environments" means editing code, swapping a file at deploy, or running a build.

  - [1mHostname detection[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m maps [38;5;153mlocation.hostname[39m to an endpoint set and resolves itself on load. Nothing to
    do at deploy; works with the plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m you have today. Costs: every environment's URL ships to the browser, and
    it breaks down if two environments share a hostname.
  - [1mInjected config script[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads a small [38;5;153mconfig.js[39m (or sets [38;5;153mwindow.APP_ENV[39m) before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; the settings
    module reads it and falls back to a default. Keeps env data out of app code, but deploy has to place the right file,
    and the HTML grows a second script tag.
  - [1mBuild-time substitution[0m — add a bundler/env-var replacement, which also unlocks real [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m. The conventional
    answer for a growing app, but it puts a toolchain and a build step into a repo that currently has neither.

  Cheap to change later: hostname → injected config is a small edit. Moving to a build step later is more work, but
  adopting it [3mnow[0m taxes every file from here on.

  My recommendation is hostname detection — it matches a repo with no build pipeline, and it's the only option where
  "change environments" requires no deploy-time ceremony at all.
